-------------------------\nChristensen, C. M. (1997). *The innovator’s dilemma: when new technologies cause great firms to fail.* Harvard Business Review Press. (https://ybe.yonsei.ac.kr/economics/community/seminar.do?articleNo=150827&attachNo=129881&mode=download)\nciteturn102search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; Christensen, C. ... (1997).*The innovator’s dilemma: when new technologies cause great firms to fail.* Harvard Business Review Press.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChristensen, C. M. 1997. The innovators dilemma . Boston: Harvard Business School Press. (https://pure.tue.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/46909422/634849-1.pdf)\nciteturn102search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Christensen, C. ... 1997.The innovators dilemma .Boston: Harvard Business School Press. ... American Social Review, 48(April): 147-160.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInnovation butterfly (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innovation_butterfly)\nciteturn102search19 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2.1 years ago; - Christensen, Clayton M.(1997).The Innovator's Dilemma : When New Technologies Cause Great Firms to Fail.Harvard Business Press.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrategic management (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_management)\nciteturn102search20 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; - Christensen, Clayton \"The Innovator's Dilemma,\" Harvard Business School Press, Boston, 1997.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVous avez dit disruption ? Enquête sur un concept flou (https://www.lemonde.fr/idees/article/2017/05/14/vous-avez-dit-disruption-enquete-sur-un-concept-flou_5127539_3232.html)\nciteturn102news28 [wordlim: 100] Published: 9.4 years ago; Cette idée a ensuite été reprise à propos de l’innovation »,</em> explique Joshua Gans, professeur de management stratégique et auteur de <em>The Disruption Dilemma</em> (MIT Press, 2016). ... Christensen, chercheur à Harvard, qui en a donné la première définition académique.La disruption, telle qu’il l’a envisagée dans son ouvrage <em>The Innovator’s ­Dilemma</em> (Harper Business, 1997), ­devient ainsi le processus par lequel une entreprise de petite envergure réussit à rivaliser avec des compagnies dûment établies, en se positionnant sur des segments de marché ­encore inexploités.\nVous avez dit disruption ? Enquête sur un concept flou\nDate: Sun May 14 13:39:08 2017 UTC\n\nUne nouvelle culture, voire de nouvelles valeurs\n\n<em>« Les origines remontent aux années 1970, époque où Ken Arrow publie </em>The Limits of Organization [non traduit, W. W. Norton &amp; Company]<em>. Il y ­explique que les entreprises ont tendance à être de plus en plus rigides dans le but d’être efficaces. Cette idée a ensuite été reprise à propos de l’innovation »,</em> explique Joshua Gans, professeur de management stratégique et auteur de <em>The Disruption Dilemma</em> (MIT Press, 2016). Si le terme a été associé à une démarche créative par le publicitaire français Jean-Marie Dru, c’est Clayton M. Christensen, chercheur à Harvard, qui en a donné la première définition académique.\nLa disruption, telle qu’il l’a envisagée dans son ouvrage <em>The Innovator’s ­Dilemma</em> (Harper Business, 1997), ­devient ainsi le processus par lequel une entreprise de petite envergure réussit à rivaliser avec des compagnies dûment établies, en se positionnant sur des segments de marché ­encore inexploités. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThese 8 Books Can Help You Finally Understand Business (https://time.com/4672139/best-business-books/)\nciteturn102news29 [wordlim: 100] Published: 9.7 years ago; These 8 Books Can Help You Finally Understand Business ... Clayton Christensen's 1997 classic The Innovators Dilemma argues that market-leading companies can become shells of their former selves if they're unwilling to \"disrupt\" themselves - because somebody else will come along and do it for them.\nThese 8 Books Can Help You Finally Understand Business\nDate: Wed Feb 22 18:13:33 2017 UTC\n\nRun rate? M&A? Non-voting shares?\nIf you've never run your own business or considered B-school, it can be tough to wrap your head around how the corporate world works. Thankfully, there's a wealth of excellent (yes, even enjoyable) tomes out there that can help. Here are eight great ones to start with, a mix of classics and new reads about today's hottest startups as suggested by writers and editors at TIME and Fortune.\nThe Innovators Dilemma ($14, Amazon)\nClayton Christensen's 1997 classic The Innovators Dilemma argues that market-leading companies can become shells of their former selves if they're unwilling to \"disrupt\" themselves - because somebody else will come along and do it for them. It's akin to a religious text in Silicon Valley, where it's viewed as a guidebook for upstart innovators seeking to take on the world's biggest firms.\nSoul of a New Machine ($10, Amazon)\n"
